    "knowledge_point": "Computing Expressions Involving Second-Order Partial Derivatives",
    "problem": "Find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).$",
    "worked_solution": "To find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)) \\\\  & =2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-y(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x) \\\\  & =2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})+2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
